WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.348 --> 00:00:05.728
Hello everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus
channel and my name is Paul.

00:00:05.728 --> 00:00:08.036
Today, I'm going to be remaking a video

00:00:08.061 --> 00:00:10.527
that I made back in the
early days of this channel

00:00:10.528 --> 00:00:14.128
because, well, a lot of the
data in that video is wrong.

00:00:14.228 --> 00:00:17.928
The topic is the 10 most widely
spoken languages in the world.

00:00:18.013 --> 00:00:20.227
We can measure the number
of speakers a language has

00:00:20.252 --> 00:00:21.941
by looking either at native speakers,

00:00:21.966 --> 00:00:24.194
or by looking at the total
number of speakers,

00:00:24.219 --> 00:00:25.668
including second language speakers.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.847
First, we'll look at the number of
native speakers for each language,

00:00:28.872 --> 00:00:32.364
and later on, we'll include second language
speakers and see how the numbers change.

00:00:32.483 --> 00:00:34.070
Based on the number of native speakers,

00:00:34.095 --> 00:00:37.187
the most widely spoken language
in the world is Mandarin.

00:00:37.188 --> 00:00:41.908
It has somewhere between 900 million
and 955 million native speakers

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:46.028
That makes it the most widely spoken
native language in the world by far

00:00:46.164 --> 00:00:49.013
Mandarin is the most widely spoken
of the Chinese languages,

00:00:49.038 --> 00:00:51.918
and it's the official language
of China and of Taiwan.

00:00:52.013 --> 00:00:54.274
The number of native speakers
does not include

00:00:54.298 --> 00:00:57.468
native speakers of other Chinese
languages, only of Mandarin.

00:00:57.528 --> 00:00:59.837
Number 2 is Spanish with somewhere between

00:00:59.862 --> 00:01:03.582
405 million and 470 million
native speakers,

00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:05.468
depending on which source you consult.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.269
Number 2 used to be English,

00:01:07.294 --> 00:01:10.864
but the number of native Spanish-speakers
has been growing much more quickly

00:01:10.889 --> 00:01:13.488
than the number of native
English-speakers in recent years.

00:01:13.644 --> 00:01:15.985
And, that brings us to
number 3 which is English,

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.708
with around 360 million native speakers.

00:01:18.866 --> 00:01:20.286
And, an interesting side note:

00:01:20.311 --> 00:01:22.206
On my previous version of this video,

00:01:22.231 --> 00:01:25.707
I got a lot of comments saying there's
no way that the number is that low

00:01:25.732 --> 00:01:29.828
because the population of the United States
alone is about 320 million.

00:01:29.853 --> 00:01:32.767
Well, keep in mind that a lot
of people in the United States,

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:34.602
a very large percentage of the population,

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:37.928
are actually immigrants who do not speak
English as their first language.

00:01:37.953 --> 00:01:41.055
And, that's also true of other countries
where English is the main language.

00:01:41.115 --> 00:01:43.868
Number 4 is Hindustani
meaning Hindi and Urdu,

00:01:43.893 --> 00:01:46.748
with around 355 million native speakers

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:51.330
Hindi and Urdu are spoken in the Indian
subcontinent in India and Pakistan,

00:01:51.355 --> 00:01:53.655
and they are very similar
in their spoken forms,

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.615
and they can be considered one language,

00:01:55.639 --> 00:01:58.248
even though their literary
forms are quite different.

00:01:58.394 --> 00:01:59.965
For more information on Hindustani,

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:02.728
check out my video on that
subject right here.

00:02:02.941 --> 00:02:07.294
Number 5 is Arabic with around
293 million native speakers.

00:02:07.319 --> 00:02:11.024
Now, I should note that Arabic
has many different spoken varieties,

00:02:11.049 --> 00:02:12.248
many different dialects,

00:02:12.248 --> 00:02:14.501
and some of them are harder
to understand than other,

00:02:14.526 --> 00:02:16.507
some of them are kind of unintelligible.

00:02:16.548 --> 00:02:17.971
For example, Moroccan Arabic,

00:02:17.996 --> 00:02:21.416
lots of people from other Arabic countries
say it's hard to understand them.

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:24.128
But, Arabic does have a standard language.

00:02:24.153 --> 00:02:26.691
Standard Arabic unites
all of the different dialects,

00:02:26.715 --> 00:02:30.333
and people can use it to communicate
with speakers of the other dialects.

00:02:30.392 --> 00:02:32.837
So, even though some of
those dialects are fairly different,

00:02:32.862 --> 00:02:34.508
they can be considered one language.

00:02:34.508 --> 00:02:39.388
If we include all varieties of Arabic,
there are 293 million native speakers.

00:02:39.588 --> 00:02:41.897
Number 6 is Portuguese with
somewhere between

00:02:41.922 --> 00:02:46.007
215 million and 225 million
native speakers.

00:02:46.088 --> 00:02:48.921
A lot of people are surprised to
hear that Portuguese is on this list,

00:02:48.946 --> 00:02:52.070
but remember Portuguese is not
only the language of Portugal.

00:02:52.095 --> 00:02:56.188
It's also spoken in Brazil,
which has a population of 200 million

00:02:56.188 --> 00:03:00.266
Number 7 is Bengali with around
206 million native speakers,

00:03:00.291 --> 00:03:01.892
mostly living in Bangladesh,

00:03:01.917 --> 00:03:04.227
and in some of the eastern
provinces of India.

00:03:04.528 --> 00:03:08.308
Number 8 is Russian with around
155 million native speakers.

00:03:08.748 --> 00:03:12.908
Number 9 is Japanese with around
126 million native speakers.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:17.208
Number 10 is Punjabi with around
102 million native speakers.

00:03:17.288 --> 00:03:20.288
It is spoken mainly in Western
India and in Pakistan.

00:03:20.368 --> 00:03:23.342
In fact, it's the most widely spoken
native language in Pakistan,

00:03:23.367 --> 00:03:25.328
even though it's not the
official language there.

00:03:25.368 --> 00:03:28.648
Those were the top ten languages based
on the number of native speakers,

00:03:28.648 --> 00:03:32.488
but the situation is somewhat different
if we include second language speakers.

00:03:32.488 --> 00:03:36.423
That's because some languages are much
more widely spoken as a lingua franca

00:03:36.448 --> 00:03:38.003
than they are as a native language.

00:03:38.138 --> 00:03:41.588
So let's see what the list looks like
if we include non-native speakers.

00:03:41.588 --> 00:03:42.787
Number one is English.

00:03:42.788 --> 00:03:46.268
English has a lot fewer native speakers
than Mandarin Chinese does,

00:03:46.268 --> 00:03:48.344
but if we include all the
people around the world

00:03:48.368 --> 00:03:51.228
who learn English as a second
language or as a foreign language,

00:03:51.228 --> 00:03:55.888
that brings the numbers up to around
1.4 to 1.5 billion speakers.

00:03:55.974 --> 00:03:58.968
Some people estimate the number
to be as high as 2 billion,

00:03:59.028 --> 00:04:02.385
but, of course, there is some disagreement
about what level of proficiency

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:05.528
is necessary in order to be included
as a speaker of English.

00:04:05.688 --> 00:04:09.727
English is the de facto global lingua
 franca, the international language.

00:04:09.928 --> 00:04:11.308
Sorry, Esperanto lovers.

00:04:11.858 --> 00:04:16.988
Number 2 is Mandarin with somewhere between
1.1 billion and 1.15 billion speakers,

00:04:16.988 --> 00:04:21.327
if we include the 190 million people
who have learned it as a second language.

00:04:21.447 --> 00:04:25.467
Those people are mostly in China and
mostly speakers of other Chinese languages.

00:04:26.728 --> 00:04:31.968
Number 3 is Spanish with somewhere between
500 million and 570 million speakers.

00:04:32.028 --> 00:04:35.296
There are about 90 million people who
speak Spanish as a second language,

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:38.588
including linguistic minorities in
a lot of Spanish-speaking countries.

00:04:38.648 --> 00:04:41.948
And there are also a lot of people who
learn Spanish as a foreign language.

00:04:42.128 --> 00:04:46.068
Number 4 is Hindustani with
around 540 million speakers.

00:04:46.248 --> 00:04:50.748
That means that there are about 200 million
second language speakers of Hindustani.

00:04:51.038 --> 00:04:55.568
Hindi and Urdu function as a lingua francas
in much of northern India and Pakistan.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.012
In Pakistan, for example,

00:04:57.037 --> 00:05:00.480
Urdu is spoken as a second language
by around 94 million people,

00:05:00.505 --> 00:05:04.008
and it's only the native language
of around 9% of the population.

00:05:04.188 --> 00:05:08.068
Number 5 is Arabic with around
422 million speakers.

00:05:08.068 --> 00:05:12.548
That means that there are around 130
million second language speakers of Arabic.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.384
A lot of these people are non-Arabs

00:05:14.409 --> 00:05:16.820
living in countries where Arabic
is an official language.

00:05:16.896 --> 00:05:19.971
For example, many speakers of
the Berber languages in North Africa

00:05:19.996 --> 00:05:21.587
learn Arabic as a second Language

00:05:21.788 --> 00:05:24.493
Varieties of Arabic are also
used as lingua francas

00:05:24.518 --> 00:05:26.010
throughout different parts of Africa.

00:05:26.073 --> 00:05:29.676
Number 6 is Russian with
around 260 million speakers.

00:05:29.882 --> 00:05:33.588
Russian jumped from number 8 on the
first list to number 6 on this list.

00:05:33.588 --> 00:05:36.292
That's because Russian is
widely spoken as a lingua franca

00:05:36.316 --> 00:05:38.448
throughout much of the former Soviet Union.

00:05:38.528 --> 00:05:39.837
For more information on Russian,

00:05:39.862 --> 00:05:42.288
check out my video on that
subject right here.

00:05:43.188 --> 00:05:48.448
Number 7 is Portuguese with between
250 million and 260 million speakers.

00:05:48.568 --> 00:05:52.068
There are about 35 million second
language speakers of Portuguese,

00:05:52.168 --> 00:05:55.166
mainly in African countries
like Angola and Mozambique,

00:05:55.191 --> 00:05:57.797
countries where Portuguese is
the former colonial language.

00:05:57.822 --> 00:06:00.360
Today, there are some native speakers
living in those countries,

00:06:00.385 --> 00:06:02.606
but Portuguese is also
used as a lingua franca

00:06:02.631 --> 00:06:04.688
between the diverse linguistic communities.

00:06:04.828 --> 00:06:08.587
Number 8 is a language that didn't even
appear on our original top 10 list,

00:06:08.588 --> 00:06:13.008
and that is the Malay language, including
Indonesian which is a variety of Malay.

00:06:13.008 --> 00:06:16.428
It is spoken by around 77 million people,
 as a native language,

00:06:16.428 --> 00:06:20.748
and there are an additional
173 million second language speakers,

00:06:20.772 --> 00:06:24.888
for a total of 250 million speakers
 of Malay and Indonesian.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.691
One of the reasons that it was spoken so
much more widely as a second language

00:06:28.715 --> 00:06:32.008
is because it is widely learned as
a second language in Indonesia.

00:06:32.008 --> 00:06:35.153
There, it is the official language,
but it's mainly used as a lingua franca

00:06:35.177 --> 00:06:37.768
because there are hundreds of
languages spoken in Indonesia

00:06:37.868 --> 00:06:41.448
Number 9 is Bengali with
around 226 million speakers.

00:06:41.588 --> 00:06:42.730
And, number 10 is French,

00:06:42.754 --> 00:06:45.868
another language that didn't appear
on our original top 10 list.

00:06:45.888 --> 00:06:48.126
While French has around
80 million native speakers,

00:06:48.151 --> 00:06:51.147
there are around 140 million
second language speakers,

00:06:51.168 --> 00:06:53.508
for a total of about 220 million.

00:06:53.568 --> 00:06:55.488
The majority of those
speakers are in Africa,

00:06:55.513 --> 00:06:58.108
where French is spoken in
around 31 different countries

00:06:58.321 --> 00:07:01.036
Some researchers project that
the Francophone population

00:07:01.061 --> 00:07:07.048
will be as high as 500 million in the year
2025, and 700 million in the year 2050.

00:07:07.288 --> 00:07:09.228
So, which list do you think
is more meaningful?

00:07:09.228 --> 00:07:13.077
I tend to think that the second list is
more meaningful because, in my opinion,

00:07:13.102 --> 00:07:16.993
the importance of a language depends on
how many people use it for communication,

00:07:17.017 --> 00:07:19.484
and not on how many
people speak it perfectly,

00:07:19.509 --> 00:07:20.969
 and with the perfect native accent.

00:07:20.994 --> 00:07:23.597
And, I think that another thing
that makes a language important

00:07:23.622 --> 00:07:25.280
is how many countries it is spoken in

00:07:25.305 --> 00:07:28.308
and over how wide of a
geographic area it is spoken.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:30.602
I think we have to balance
these two different factors

00:07:30.627 --> 00:07:33.048
in order to determine how
important the language is.

00:07:33.197 --> 00:07:36.221
So, even though Mandarin has the
largest number of native speakers,

00:07:36.245 --> 00:07:39.268
and even though it has the second
largest number of total speakers,

00:07:39.448 --> 00:07:41.017
it might not be the most important

00:07:41.041 --> 00:07:43.328
or second most important
language in the world.

00:07:43.396 --> 00:07:45.923
The most important language
in the world is, right now,

00:07:45.948 --> 00:07:49.698
clearly English because it is widely spoken
by so many people around the world.

00:07:49.833 --> 00:07:53.454
I would say that number 2 is Spanish
because it's spoken by a large population,

00:07:53.478 --> 00:07:56.168
and it's spoken in dozens of
countries around the world.

00:07:56.288 --> 00:07:59.108
And, because the Spanish-speaking
population is growing quickly.

00:07:59.108 --> 00:08:01.727
I would say that French might
be another one in the top 5

00:08:01.752 --> 00:08:04.428
because of its fairly large
total number of speakers,

00:08:04.468 --> 00:08:06.903
because it's widely spoken
in Europe and Africa,

00:08:06.928 --> 00:08:09.868
and because of the projected growth
in the Francophone population.

00:08:10.076 --> 00:08:13.028
Another might be Arabic because
it's spoken throughout many countries

00:08:13.053 --> 00:08:14.503
in the Middle East and North Africa,

00:08:14.528 --> 00:08:17.448
and by a growing population in
Europe and around the world.

00:08:17.556 --> 00:08:19.257
And, another one is probably Chinese,

00:08:19.282 --> 00:08:22.076
even though it's not an official
language in many places,

00:08:22.172 --> 00:08:25.771
China is an important country with
 a huge population and a large economy,

00:08:25.795 --> 00:08:28.592
and Chinese might become more
important in the future.

00:08:28.703 --> 00:08:30.814
So, the question of the day is:
What do you think?

00:08:30.838 --> 00:08:32.948
What are the most important
languages in the world?

00:08:33.048 --> 00:08:36.148
Leave your opinions and your reasons
in the comments down below.

00:08:36.368 --> 00:08:39.768
Be sure to follow Langfocus
on Twitter, on Instagram and on Facebook.

00:08:39.828 --> 00:08:42.757
And again, I'd like to say thank you
to all of my patreon supporters

00:08:42.782 --> 00:08:44.170
for your continuous support.

00:08:44.403 --> 00:08:47.296
Thank you for watching,
and have a nice day!

